{
  "id": "retrieval-small",
  "version": "v1",
  "about": "A DELIBERATELY SMALL world — 20 facts about one owner — with 62 queries carrying the same graded relevance judgments and the same category taxonomy as retrieval-core.v1. It exists for one purpose: the dense lane's DENSE_MARGIN_FLOOR was swept against a 60-fact corpus, and the margin statistic is not scale-free. Measured on the retrieval-core fixture, the answerable p10 margin climbs 0.037 -> 0.092 and the median 0.079 -> 0.191 as the corpus grows 15 -> 60, while the silence p90 sits near 0.10 throughout. A threshold read at one size cannot be assumed at another, so retrieval currently declines to run the dense lane below 60 facts. This corpus is the second calibration point that decides whether one floor holds, or whether the floor has to be a function of corpus size.",
  "limitations": [
    "AUTHORED BY THE SAME PROCESS THAT TUNES THE THRESHOLD IT VALIDATES. That is the central risk here and it is not fully mitigable: a corpus written with the failure modes in mind can flatter them. Three things were done against it. The domain is deliberately unrelated to retrieval-core (a ceramics studio, not a fintech startup) so phrasings are not being reused. Query wording was written before the labels were assigned. The silence queries ask about categories the corpus genuinely never mentions, rather than near-misses chosen to be hard.",
    "20 facts is small enough that a single fact carries 5% of recall. Per-category rates move in coarse steps and should be read as directional, not precise.",
    "A calibration corpus, NOT a quality benchmark. It answers 'does the margin floor transfer across corpus size'. It does not measure whether retrieval is good — retrieval-core does that, on a world with enough facts for the metrics to have resolution.",
    "The same owner throughout, like retrieval-core. Multi-owner isolation is covered elsewhere.",
    "No temporal supersession chains longer than two, because 20 facts cannot carry them without the corpus becoming mostly bookkeeping."
  ],
